id: decisions/041-dot-yawn-is-the-human-grammar
title: "One grammar behind the .yawn extension"
kind: decision-frame
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; drafted from the JUDGMENTS.md reversal axis 'serialisation-form'; recommendation is an attributed inference and ratifies nothing"
version: "1.0.0"
updated: 2026-09-12
template_ref: templates/decision.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave

axis: serialisation-form
axis_gloss: "whether a record is written in an exact machine grammar (JSON, schema-shaped, hashed) or a flat human-writable one"
poles:
  a: structured-machine-format
  b: flat-human-format
split: leaning
draft_status: full
leverage: 6.3
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(9) x split_weight(0.7) x authored_conflict_bonus(1)"

question: >
  Is a .yawn file always the human-readable grammar, with machine contracts
  living under .json, or may a .yawn file be JSON when a schema validates it?

why_it_matters: >
  core/canonical-extension.yawn claims the extension identifies one
  human-readable grammar, and the corpus falsifies it: the only two templates
  with executable contract validation are JSON, and the in-flight proof block
  now lists media_role as not_covered and points here. Once answered, a bot
  opening any .yawn knows whether it needs one parser or two, and a child Yawn
  that wants a schema knows whether it must write YAML and point at the schema
  or may write the schema's own serialization.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    7 judgments sit at structured-machine-format (0 authored) and 2 at
    flat-human-format (0 authored). The repository asserts both directions on this
    axis.
  attributed_to: TAXONOMY.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    Whether Dave sees JSON-in-.yawn as a mistake to reverse or as the intended
    home for machine contracts, and whether the front-matter-plus-prose form is a
    third grammar or a header variant.

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      rule: A record that has a schema is written in the schema's own serialization
      (JSON, camelCase), .yawn extension included; the human-readable form is a
      rendering, not the record. canonical.media_role in
      core/canonical-extension.yawn is changed to 'schema-shaped protocol record'.
    cost: "Rewrites the media_role claim and relationship_to_identity in core/canonical-extension.yawn; over time every record with a schema (thirty schemas exist) migrates to JSON."
    risk: "The repo's stated reason to exist (inspectable orientation a person can read and correct) is traded for validation; yawn.yawn first_move ('copy basic.yawn') stops being true for schema-backed records."
    reversibility: false
    proof_needed: "tests/observation-question-art-v1.test.mjs already validates the two JSON templates; extend it so every template that names a schema is JSON. Today thirty of thirty-two would fail."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0041"
      - "J0350"
      - "J0538"
      - "J0637"
      - "J0837"
      - "J1157"
  - name: B
    description: >
      rule: Every .yawn file is the flat human grammar (YAML-shaped, snake_case,
      id/title/kind-led). Machine contracts live under .json
      (templates/art-brief.v1.json, templates/question-proposal.v1.json) and a .yawn
      record points at them; a hash binding is a field inside a human-readable
      record, never the file's grammar. Multi-valued enum fields hold a list, not
      pipe-joined text.
    cost: "Rename two templates and every path that cites them (tests, yawn.yawn template_links, schemas); convert interface/desktop-homebase-v1.yawn's front-matter form or register it; fix records/access-log.yawn entries."
    risk: "The two renamed contracts lose the .yawn identity the repo gave them on purpose and their tests must be re-pointed."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Extend tests/record-shape.test.mjs (in-flight): no .yawn file anywhere in the tree may begin with '{'; extend scripts/validate-canonical-extension.mjs so canonical.media_role moves from not_covered to covers."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0428"
      - "J0632"
  - name: C
    description: >
      rule: A .yawn file is the human-readable grammar EXCEPT the two json-contract
      templates already registered in templates/node.yawn record_shapes
      (art-brief.v1, question-proposal.v1), grandfathered by name with a ceiling of
      two that may only shrink; no new JSON-bodied .yawn may be added, and a new
      machine contract is written as .json beside a .yawn that points at it. The
      sha256 binding in
      records/interception-statement-image-approval-2026-08-29.yawn is the model:
      structure as a field inside a readable record. Multi-valued enum fields hold a
      list.
    cost: "One ledger in a test, one sentence in templates/node.yawn record_shapes.rule (already half-written), one fix to records/access-log.yawn."
    risk: "Two grammars remain forever if the ledger never shrinks; readers of those two files still need a second parser."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "Extend tests/record-shape.test.mjs: any .yawn anywhere whose first non-blank character is '{' must be listed as json-contract in templates/node.yawn with ceiling 2; name that test under core/canonical-extension.yawn proof.covers for canonical.media_role; add an assertion that entry_shape enum fields in records/*.yawn hold one value or a list."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0637"
      - "J0837"
      - "J1157"
      - "J0428"
      - "J0632"

recommendation:
  option_ref: C
  confidence: 0.65
  epistemic_status: inferred
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    No judgment on this axis is authored, so the stated rule has to come from
    outside it: core/canonical-extension.yawn's media_role 'human-readable
    protocol record' (GROUND_TRUTH FACT 9) is authored and is the claim the two
    JSON files break, and the in-flight templates/node.yawn registry already ranks
    header canonical and labels json-contract as not human-readable-first. The
    structured side's strongest evidence, the proof-attached sha256 binding, is
    not in conflict at all: it is structure inside a readable record, which is
    what C makes the rule. B is cleaner but re-points tests and links for two
    files; C freezes the exception at two and finally gives media_role a proof.
    Close between B and C.

target_condition:
  mode: decide
  proposed_by: agent-on-behalf
  affected_principals:
    - principal:dave
  ratification_status: proposed
  sufficient_when:
    - "The count of JSON-bodied .yawn files in the tree is two and frozen by name, or zero."
    - "canonical.media_role appears under proof.covers in core/canonical-extension.yawn with the test that enforces it named."
    - "records/access-log.yawn entries hold one action value or a list, never pipe-joined text, and a test asserts it."
    - "interface/desktop-homebase-v1.yawn is either registered as a shape or converted to the header form."

choice:
  selected_by: ""
  option_ref: ""
  authority_ref: ""

proof:
  covers:
    - target_condition
    - options
  falsifier: >
    A third JSON-bodied .yawn lands, or media_role is still listed under
    not_covered a quarter after ratification, while npm test stays green.
  status: proposed

evidence_sample:
  - "J0041"
  - "J0350"
  - "J0538"
  - "J0637"
  - "J0837"
  - "J1157"
  - "J0428"
  - "J0632"

boundary:
  - "confidence cannot ratify an objective; this record proposes and Dave decides"
  - "the REVEALED judgments cited here describe what artifacts do, not what Dave chose"

source:
  template: templates/decision.yawn
  axis: TAXONOMY.md
  inventory: JUDGMENTS.md
  id_index: TAXONOMY.md
